 ## Architecture
 
 The record store exposes authenticated submission, current-state retrieval, submission-history retrieval, and review-outcome operations. Persistence keeps original submissions, their authors, and server receipt times. A submission carries an idempotency key so a connection failure does not create another edit on retry.
 
-A visit's scientific fields include observation date, observer list, species cover estimates, and notes. Some edits are unrelated, such as correcting an observer name and adding a missing species. Other edits are related, such as revising a species identification and its cover estimate. The server cannot infer all such relationships from field names.
+A visit's scientific fields include observation date, observer list, species cover estimates, and notes. Every submission carries the complete visit payload: the server never combines fields from different submissions. Which of several competing revisions is current is a human decision made in review, not a server-side merge.
 
-The selected concurrency policy will define the remaining submission metadata and review operations. They are deliberately not specified yet.
+## Revision Model
+
+Every accepted submission creates a revision of the visit, identified by a server-assigned revision ID unique within the visit. Revisions are ordered by acceptance time.
+
+- The first accepted submission for a visit creates revision 1, which becomes the current revision.
+- Every later submission names its base revision: the retained revision whose payload the worker started from.
+- A submission whose base revision is the current revision is accepted onto the resolved line and becomes the new current revision.
+- A submission whose base revision is any other retained revision of the visit is retained as a competing revision and marks the visit unresolved. Arrival order between concurrent workers confers no authority: both orders of the same two submissions produce one current revision and one competing revision.
+
+While a visit is unresolved, current-state retrieval continues to return the current revision's payload with the unresolved flag set; field workers keep working from the resolved line, and competing revisions are visible only through review. A visit can accumulate several competing revisions before it is resolved.
 
 ## Submission and Retrieval
 
-Authenticate the submitting worker and validate field types and plot references before accepting a submission. Preserve the original payload separately from any later resolved or combined representation. A retry with the same key and identical payload returns the original acceptance result; reuse with a different payload is rejected.
+Authenticate the submitting worker and validate field types and the plot reference against the catalog before accepting a submission. A plot UUID must resolve in the catalog, whether active or archived. Preserve the original payload of every submission separately from any later resolution outcome.
+
+Submission envelope:
+
+| Field | Requirement |
+|---|---|
+| visit UUID | Client-generated; identifies the visit |
+| idempotency key | Client-generated; unique per submission attempt |
+| base revision ID | Required for every submission to an existing visit; must be a retained revision of that visit. Omitted only on a visit's first submission. |
+| visit payload | Complete scientific fields: observation date, observer list, species cover estimates, notes, plot UUID |
+
+A submission to an existing visit without a base revision, or naming a revision that belongs to another visit, is rejected as invalid. A retry with the same idempotency key and identical payload returns the original acceptance result, including the assigned revision ID and whether that revision is current. Reuse of an accepted key with a different payload is rejected.
+
+Current-state retrieval returns the current revision's payload, the current revision ID, and an unresolved flag. When the flag is set, the response also lists each competing revision with its revision ID, base revision ID, author, and server receipt time. History retrieval returns every retained submission with its original payload, author, and receipt time regardless of resolution outcome; resolved-away revisions are never removed from history.
+
+## Resolution
+
+A coordinator resolves an unresolved visit by selecting one candidate: the current revision or any competing revision. The selected revision becomes the new current revision. The resolution records the coordinator's identity, the selected revision ID, and every competing revision ID the resolution disposes of. Unselected revisions remain in history; no submitted payload is ever deleted or overwritten.
+
+After a resolution, a later submission naming a superseded revision as its base is simply a new competing revision against the new current revision — the same rule as any other stale base, with no special handling.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
 
-Retrieval must distinguish a usable current state from unresolved review work. Exactly how those states are represented remains part of the open choice.
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Concurrent edit policy | Whole-visit revisions: each edit names its base revision; overlapping edits become competing revisions for coordinator review | Field-aware three-way merge (tablets keep base snapshots, send structured changes; disjoint field edits combine automatically) | Merge rules can combine scientifically related edits — a revised species identification and its cover estimate — without anyone noticing. The team accepts a larger review queue over maintaining merge rules that cross scientific relationships invisibly. Complete visits also match how a worker thinks about a submitted visit. |
+| Resolution shape | Coordinator selects one submitted revision as current | Coordinator composes a merged record during resolution | Composition reintroduces the merge rules the concurrency decision avoids, just through the review door. Selection keeps every current record identical to some worker's actual submission, and keeps the review workflow explainable. |
 
-## Open Design Choice: Concurrent Visit Edits
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
 
-OPEN: select the concurrency policy before completing this node's public contract.
+### Resolved
 
-Two approaches remain credible:
+1. ✅ Concurrent edit policy: whole-visit revisions (see Decisions & Alternatives).
 
-- Whole-visit revisions: each edit names its starting revision. Concurrent edits produce competing complete revisions for a coordinator to resolve. This preserves the worker's submitted visit as a unit and needs relatively little merge logic, but even edits to unrelated fields create review work. Tablet-sync would retain base revision IDs and display pending competing revisions. Review-export would need a revision comparison workflow and exclude unresolved visits from its export set.
-- Field-aware three-way merge: each edit supplies its base state and changes. Disjoint field changes combine automatically; overlapping changes require review. This reduces review volume and gets more visits ready for export, but requires maintained rules for scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries. Automatically combined fields can also cross scientific relationships the rules do not understand. Tablet-sync would retain base snapshots and send structured changes. Review-export would need field-level provenance and a review view for overlapping changes.
+### Deferred
 
-The choice trades review workload and submission size against implementation burden and preservation of whole-visit intent. Neither approach may discard original submissions. Field-aware merging is not ruled out by a requirement that every combined record receive manual approval.
+1. Whether a resolution outcome carries a coordinator note explaining the choice — deferred to review-export's design of the coordinator interface.
 
